%U A401527 4,5,4,5,8,8,6,6,2,0,0,4,9,5,6,5,8,2,1,4,3,9,7,1,2,4,1,3,3,9,6,3,6,1,9,9,4
%N A401527 Decimal expansion of liminf_{N->oo} log(N)*(P(N) - log_10(2)), where P(N) is the probability that a random integer k in 1..N, chosen with probability proportional to 1/k, has leading decimal digit 1.
%C A401527 By Duncan's theorem, P(N) -> log_10(2) (A007524) as N -> oo (Benford's law for the leading digit 1). Moreover P(N) > log_10(2) for every N >= 1 (Khomyakov, Theorem 3.1(ii) with t = 2): the leading digit 1 is more frequent than under Benford's law for every N, not only asymptotically.
%C A401527 log(N)*(P(N) - log_10(2)) oscillates between this constant (its liminf, the limit along N = 10^K - 1) and this constant + log(2)*log_10(5) = 0.64599099158549304876... (its limsup, the limit along N = 2*10^K - 1) (Khomyakov, Theorem 4.3(a) with t = 2).
%H A401527 R. L. Duncan, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00150517.1969.12431129">Note on the initial digit problem</a>, Fibonacci Quart. 7 (1969), 474-475.
%H A401527 Vladimir Khomyakov, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23660786">The harmonic leading-digit law is one-sided at every N</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A401527 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benford%27s_law">Benford's law</a>.
%F A401527 Equals Sum_{j>=0} (psi(2*10^j) - psi(10^j) - log(2)) - gamma*log_10(2), where psi is the digamma function and gamma is Euler's constant (A001620).
%F A401527 Equals Sum_{j>=0} (H(2*10^j - 1) - H(10^j - 1) - log(2)) - gamma*log_10(2), where H(n) is the n-th harmonic number.
%F A401527 Equals Sum_{j>=0} (psi(2*10^j) - (1-x)*psi(10^j) - x*psi(10^(j+1))), where x = log_10(2).
%e A401527 0.161501903784008866445733564093950536878366185207891919574115...
%o A401527 (Python) # via mpmath; M = Sum_{j>=0} (psi(2*10^j) - psi(10^j) - log(2)) - gamma*log_10(2)
%o A401527 from mpmath import mp, mpf, log, digamma, euler
%o A401527 digits = 105          # number of digits to compute; the only parameter to change
%o A401527 mp.dps = digits + 20  # working precision: 20 guard digits
%o A401527 J = digits + 20       # number of terms; the omitted tail is in [0, 10^-J)
%o A401527 M = sum(digamma(2*mpf(10)**j) - digamma(mpf(10)**j) - log(2) for j in range(J)) - euler*log(2)/log(10)
%o A401527 print(", ".join(str(int(M*10**digits))))  # first `digits` digits of M = 0.1615..., truncated
%Y A401527 Cf. A401525, A401526, A001620, A007524.
%K A401527 nonn,cons,new
%O A401527 0,2
%A A401527 _Vladimir Khomyakov_, Oct 28 2026
